From: Billy Moses <wmoses@google.com>
Subject: [XLA:CPU] Cap the size of loop fusions (operands / fused instructions)

CpuInstructionFusion has no limit on how large a loop fusion may grow. The only
size-related guard, FusionNodeIndexingEvaluation::CodeDuplicationTooHigh, is
skipped entirely when the pass runs with may_duplicate=false, which is what
cpu_compiler.cc selects whenever xla_cpu_use_multi_output_fusion is set. The
result on reverse-mode AD programs of stencil codes (Enzyme-MLIR/Reactant on
Oceananigans) are loop fusions with 210-250 operands and 880-1045 fused
instructions per output element: the adjoint of every stencil tap gets pulled
into the pad-accumulate chain it feeds. The elemental emitter then produces
per-element code with hundreds of live inputs and bounds-checked pad paths that
neither vectorizes nor fits in registers; such a fusion runs several times
slower than materializing its taps.

Add a size budget, checked independently of may_duplicate: do not fuse a
producer into an existing fusion if the fusion would end up with more than
`xla_cpu_max_fusion_operands` operands (default 96, the same budget the GPU
backend uses in FusionFitsInBudget) or more than `xla_cpu_max_fused_instructions`
fused instructions (default 512). Both are overridable through
xla_backend_extra_options, and a value <= 0 disables the corresponding check.

On the Oceananigans channel-model gradient (8 time steps, one x86 core) the
equivalent restructuring (materializing the pad operands) takes the gradient
from 29.0 s to 15.4 s with bit-identical results up to round-off (rel. L2 diff
of the gradient fields <= 2e-15).

